menu "MBUS Simulator"

config SIM_MBUS_TX_GPIO
    int "Meter-link UART2 TX GPIO"
    default 17
    help
        GPIO17 is U2TXD's default routing on the classic ESP32, and it is what
        the interface board is wired to. It is a Kconfig option rather than a
        literal because the pin is not free on every module: WROVER variants
        wire GPIO16 and GPIO17 to the SPI PSRAM, where gpio_config() and
        uart_set_pin() both succeed and the pin stays dead. This board is a
        WROOM, so they are available. FSD §2.1.

config SIM_TX_INVERT
    bool "Invert the meter-link TX signal"
    default n
    help
        The polarity at **boot**. Off, because this rig's output signal is not
        inverted: UART idle drives the pin high, which the driver stage passes
        through as the 24 V M-Bus mark state. Measured, not assumed — with
        inversion on, a continuous 0x55 came off the line as a uniform 0xD5,
        the exact byte a one-inversion mismatch produces.

        You do not need to change this to test the other polarity: the console
        command `invert on|off` flips it at runtime for the session
        (FR-LNK-06). Change it here only when the *rig* is permanently wired to
        an inverting driver stage. FSD FR-LNK-02/06.

config SIM_LINE_SENSE_GPIO
    int "Line-sense feedback GPIO (-1 for none)"
    default -1
    help
        Optional input wired back from the *driver stage's output* — not from
        the TX pin — so the firmware can timestamp the moment the line actually
        leaves idle and report it as lead_us (FSD FR-FLT-17).

        This is a wiring fact, not a test parameter, which is why it lives here
        rather than on the console. With no such wire the firmware knows only
        when it handed bytes to the UART, which is not the same quantity and
        must not be reported as if it were: lead_us then says n/a.

config SIM_PUSH_PERIOD_SECONDS
    int "Seconds between telegrams"
    default 5
    range 5 60
    help
        The E450's Consumer Information 1 list is pushed every 5 s, which is the
        cadence this simulator exists to reproduce, so 5 is the default and the
        faithful value. Raise it only for a rig that wants a wider silent gap
        between transmissions than 5 s leaves.

        Must divide 60: FR-SCH-01 aligns emission to the wall clock's seconds
        field, so a period that does not divide 60 emits unevenly across the
        minute boundary. app_main refuses to run with one that does not, rather
        than emitting a schedule nobody asked for. FSD FR-SCH-01/08.

config SIM_EPOCH
    string "Synthetic clock epoch"
    default "2026-01-01T00:00:00"
    help
        The clock the device holds at boot. There is no RTC and no time source,
        by design: determinism is worth more here than truth, because output
        that changes run to run cannot be compared. FSD FR-CLK-01.

endmenu
